    "stimulus": {
      "title": "Saturday at Willow Bay",
      "text": "On the first Saturday of each month, the Willow Bay Volunteers meet at the small beach beside the river. The group began two years ago when local resident Mara Iles noticed plastic bottles collecting near the boat ramp after heavy rain. At first, six neighbours came with bags and gloves. Now, around thirty people usually attend, including families, students, and two shop owners.\n\nThe volunteers arrive at 9 a.m. and collect litter for about ninety minutes. They work in pairs because some rubbish is hidden among rocks or under wet leaves. Afterward, they sort what they have found into glass, metal, plastic, and general waste. The town recycling team collects the separate bags on Monday.\n\nCleaning the beach is not the group’s whole purpose. Before each event, Mara gives a short talk about avoiding single-use packaging and keeping rubbish out of drains. Children receive a simple map and mark places where they find the most litter. The group shares this information with the town council, which recently placed two new bins near the boat ramp.\n\nLast month, the volunteers found less plastic than usual but more small pieces of fishing line. Mara called this a mixed result: the beach looked cleaner, yet the line could harm birds. She thanked everyone for returning each month and said that steady effort was more useful than one large clean-up. At the end, the group has tea at a nearby café and plans the next visit.",
      "table": null
    },
